tch_internal_sprite_less_than_or_equal` | no (matches C) |\n| `spritebatch_internal_merge_sort_iteration` | `spritebatch_internal_merge_sort_iteration` | no (matches C) |\n| `spritebatch_internal_merge_sort_recurse` | `spritebatch_internal_merge_sort_recurse` | no (matches C) |\n\n### Undefined (imported) symbols\n\nC `.so` imports: `memcpy@GLIBC_2.14` plus the usual weak CRT stubs\n(`_ITM_deregisterTMCloneTable`, `_ITM_registerTMCloneTable`,\n`__cxa_finalize`, `__gmon_start__`).\nThe Rust `.so` imports only libc/CRT symbols (`memcpy`, unwinder/allocator\nstubs). **0 missing/undefined non-libc symbols.**\n\n### Data types crossing the FFI boundary\n\n| C | Rust | size / align (x86-64) |\n|---|------|------------------------|\n| `struct spritebatch_sprite_t { unsigned long long texture_id; int sort_bits; }` | `#[repr(C)] struct spritebatch_sprite_t { texture_id: c_ulonglong, sort_bits: c_int }` | 16 bytes, align 8, 4 trailing padding bytes |\n\n## Feature combinations\n\n`translation/Cargo.toml` declares **no `[features]` section**, so the only\nbuild configuration is the default one (`--no-default-features` is equivalent).\nSee `CONFIGS.md` for the runtime configuration surface, which is where all the\nreal variation lives.\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_xt6tp5eeutrqusmp3hiuytqsr7of2x7kahxu4ctenvvz2hmseoea","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_bdrk_01HDsuYMZvpUypQJL3XLctz3","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-pgHR6S/translation/ERRORS.md","content":"# ERRORS.md — Phase C error / rejection surface table\n\nMechanically derived from `c_src/src/lib.c` and `c_src/include/lib.h`.\n\n## Mechanical grep results\n\n```\n$ grep -nE 'return|assert|NULL|<|>|if' c_src/src/lib.c\n```\n\n* `return 1;`  x2, `return 0;` x1  — in `spritebatch_internal_sprite_less_than_or_equal`\n* `return;`    x1 — the `hi - lo <= 1` early-out in `spritebatch_internal_merge_sort_recurse`\n* `assert`     — **0 occurrences**\n* `NULL` / null checks — **0 occurrences**\n* explicit range / bounds checks — **0 occurrences**\n* error enums, error codes, `errno`, `RETURN_ERROR`-style macros — **0 occurrences**\n* min/max constants — **0 occurrences**\n\n**Conclusion:** the public API is `void merge_sort(...)`. It has *no error\nchannel at all*: no return value, no out-parameter status, no sentinel, no\nassertion. Therefore every row below is a *rejection-by-early-return* or an\n*undefined-behaviour / crash* boundary rather than an error code, and the\ndifferential assertion is \"same observable effect\" (same memory state, or same\nfatal signal) rather than \"same error code\".\n\n## Error-surface table\n\n| # | function | trigger (exact invalid input/condition) | expected C result | status |\n|---|----------|------------------------------------------|-------------------|--------|\n| 1 | `merge_sort` | `size == 0`, `a`/`b` valid non-null | `memcpy(b,a,0)` is a no-op; `recurse(b,0,0,a)` hits `hi-lo == 0 <= 1` and returns. Both buffers left bit-for-bit unchanged. No write, no read. | [x] |\n| 2 | `merge_sort` | `size == 0`, `a == NULL && b == NULL` | `memcpy(NULL,NULL,0)` performs no access; early-out as row 1. Returns normally, no fault. | [x] |\n| 3 | `merge_sort` | `size == 1` | `memcpy` copies exactly 16 bytes `a -> b`; `recurse` early-outs (`hi-lo == 1 <= 1`). `a` unchanged, `b == a`. Never dereferences element 1. | [x] |\n| 4 | `spritebatch_internal_merge_sort_recurse` (via `merge_sort`) | `hi - lo <= 1` (the only guard in the file) — reached for every leaf of the recursion | `return;` immediately, no write to either buffer | [x] |\n| 5 | `spritebatch_internal_sprite_less_than_or_equal` | `a->sort_bits > b->sort_bits` (the only path that reaches `return 0`) | returns `0`. NOTE: the second `if` (`sort_bits == ... && texture_id <= ...`) is **dead code** — the first `if` already returns 1 for `==`. So `texture_id` NEVER affects ordering. Replicated verbatim. | [x] |\n| 6 | `spritebatch_internal_sprite_less_than_or_equal` | `a->sort_bits == INT_MIN`, `b->sort_bits == INT_MAX` and the reverse (extreme signed operands) | plain signed `<=`, no subtraction, so no overflow: `INT_MIN <= INT_MAX` -> 1, `INT_MAX <= INT_MIN` -> 0 | [x] |\n| 7 | `merge_sort` | `size < 0` (e.g. `-1`) | `sizeof(t) * (size_t)size` = `16 * 0xFFFF...FFFF` (mod 2^64) = a ~2^64 byte count -> `memcpy` faults. **Fatal signal (SIGSEGV).** Rust must fault identically. | [x] |\n| 8 | `merge_sort` | `size > 0` but `a == NULL` and/or `b == NULL` (null pointer with non-zero length) | `memcpy` dereferences NULL -> **fatal signal (SIGSEGV)**. Rust must fault identically. | [x] |\n| 9 | `merge_sort` | `size` larger than the actual allocation (oversized length, e.g. `size = 64` on a 4-element buffer) | out-of-bounds read+write; deterministic within a large enough over-allocated arena, so the two implementations must produce identical bytes over the whole arena. | [x] |\n| 10 | `merge_sort` | `a == b` (aliased buffers) | `memcpy` with `dst == src` (UB per standard, no-op in glibc), then the merge reads and writes the same storage. Must match byte-for-byte. | [x] |\n| 11 | `merge_sort` | `size == INT_MIN` (extreme negative, sign bit only) | `(size_t)INT_MIN * 16` wraps to `0x8000000000000000 * ...` -> huge -> `memcpy` faults. **Fatal signal.** | [x] |\n| 12 | `merge_sort` | out-of-range \"enum\"-like third argument: the C prototype takes `int`, so any 32-bit value is accepted. Values with no meaningful interpretation (`INT_MAX`) | `16 * INT_MAX` bytes -> allocation-sized fault. **Fatal signal.** (There are no true C enums in this API; `size:int` is the only integer input, so this row covers the \"any int is a legal FFI value\" class.) | [x] |\n\n## Notes on how the fatal-signal rows are tested\n\nRows 7, 8, 11 and 12 are *supposed* to crash. They are tested by `fork()`ing a\nchild, calling the C function in the child, `waitpid`ing for the terminating\nsignal, then doing the same for the Rust function, and asserting the two\nchildren died the **same way** (same `WIFSIGNALED` / signal number, or both\nexited cleanly). This is a genuine differential assertion, not \"both failed\nsomehow\".\n"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":879,"cache_read_input_tokens":22353,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":879,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"62db9202-04ec-4326-944d-d3d0b591b984","uuid":"7f4ef1d6-037b-4705-a0eb-7f58426cc549","timestamp":"2026-09-05T01:08:53.501Z"}
